[{"order":1,"type":"heading","text":"Canvas API: Drawing Shapes, Text, and Images"},{"order":2,"type":"subheading","text":"Introduction to the HTML5 Canvas API"},{"order":3,"type":"paragraph","text":"The HTML5 Canvas API provides a powerful way to draw graphics on a web page using JavaScript. It's a versatile tool for creating everything from simple shapes and text to complex animations and interactive games. This tutorial will guide you through the fundamentals of using the Canvas API to draw shapes, text, and images."},{"order":4,"type":"subheading","text":"Setting Up Your Canvas"},{"order":5,"type":"paragraph","text":"Before you can start drawing, you need to create a `<canvas>` element in your HTML and get a 2D rendering context using JavaScript."},{"order":6,"type":"code","text":"<canvas id="myCanvas" width="500" height="300" style="border:1px solid #d3d3d3;">
Your browser does not support the HTML5 canvas tag.
</canvas>"},{"order":7,"type":"paragraph","text":"Now, let's get the canvas element and its 2D rendering context in JavaScript."},{"order":8,"type":"code","text":"const canvas = document.getElementById('myCanvas');
const ctx = canvas.getContext('2d');

if (ctx) {
  // Canvas is supported, you can start drawing here
} else {
  alert('Canvas is not supported in your browser.');
}"},{"order":9,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `getContext('2d')` method returns a drawing context that allows you to draw 2D shapes, text, and images on the canvas."},{"order":10,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawing Shapes"},{"order":11,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Canvas API provides several methods for drawing basic shapes, such as rectangles, circles, and lines."},{"order":12,"type":"subheading","text":"Rectangles"},{"order":13,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can draw rectangles using the `fillRect()`, `strokeRect()`, and `clearRect()` methods."},{"order":14,"type":"code","text":"// Filled rectangle
ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
ctx.fillRect(50, 50, 100, 50); // x, y, width, height

// Stroked rectangle
ctx.strokeStyle = 'blue';
ctx.strokeRect(200, 50, 100, 50);

// Clear a rectangle
ctx.clearRect(350, 50, 100, 50);"},{"order":15,"type":"subheading","text":"Paths (Lines, Circles, Arcs)"},{"order":16,"type":"paragraph","text":"Paths are sequences of points connected by lines. You can create complex shapes using paths."},{"order":17,"type":"code","text":"// Line
ctx.beginPath();
ctx.moveTo(50, 150); // Starting point
ctx.lineTo(150, 150); // Ending point
ctx.strokeStyle = 'green';
ctx.stroke();

// Circle
ctx.beginPath();
ctx.arc(250, 175, 25, 0, 2 * Math.PI); // x, y, radius, startAngle, endAngle
ctx.fillStyle = 'orange';
ctx.fill();

// Arc
ctx.beginPath();
ctx.arc(350, 175, 25, 0, Math.PI / 2); // x, y, radius, startAngle, endAngle
ctx.stroke();"},{"order":18,"type":"paragraph","text":"Important path methods include `beginPath()`, `moveTo()`, `lineTo()`, `arc()`, `closePath()`, `fill()`, and `stroke()`."},{"order":19,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawing Text"},{"order":20,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Canvas API allows you to draw text on the canvas using the `fillText()` and `strokeText()` methods. You can also customize the text's font, size, and alignment."},{"order":21,"type":"code","text":"ctx.font = '20px Arial';
ctx.fillStyle = 'black';
ctx.fillText('Hello Canvas!', 50, 250);

ctx.font = '30px Times New Roman';
ctx.strokeStyle = 'purple';
ctx.strokeText('Stroke Text', 50, 290);"},{"order":22,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `font` property sets the font style, size, and family. `fillText()` draws filled text, and `strokeText()` draws outlined text."},{"order":23,"type":"subheading","text":"Drawing Images"},{"order":24,"type":"paragraph","text":"You can draw images on the canvas using the `drawImage()` method. This method can take an `<img>` element, a `<canvas>` element, or a `<video>` element as its source."},{"order":25,"type":"code","text":"// Load an image
const img = new Image();
img.src = 'https://via.placeholder.com/50'; // Replace with your image URL

img.onload = function() {
  // Draw the image once it's loaded
  ctx.drawImage(img, 400, 250);
};
"},{"order":26,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `drawImage()` method can also be used to scale and crop images.  For example, `ctx.drawImage(img, sx, sy, sWidth, sHeight, dx, dy, dWidth, dHeight)` allows you to specify a source rectangle (sx, sy, sWidth, sHeight) from the image and draw it onto the canvas at a destination rectangle (dx, dy, dWidth, dHeight)."},{"order":27,"type":"subheading","text":"Styling and Transformations"},{"order":28,"type":"paragraph","text":"The Canvas API provides several properties and methods for styling and transforming your drawings."},{"order":29,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`fillStyle`: Sets the fill color."},{"order":30,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`strokeStyle`: Sets the stroke color."},{"order":31,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`lineWidth`: Sets the width of lines."},{"order":32,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`lineCap`: Sets the style of line endings (butt, round, square)."},{"order":33,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`lineJoin`: Sets the style of line joins (round, bevel, miter)."},{"order":34,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`shadowColor`, `shadowBlur`, `shadowOffsetX`, `shadowOffsetY`:  Adds shadows to shapes and text."},{"order":35,"type":"paragraph","text":"Transformations allow you to scale, rotate, translate, and skew your drawings."},{"order":36,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`translate(x, y)`: Moves the canvas origin to (x, y)."},{"order":37,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`rotate(angle)`: Rotates the canvas by `angle` radians."},{"order":38,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`scale(x, y)`: Scales the canvas by factors `x` and `y`."},{"order":39,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`transform(a, b, c, d, e, f)`: Applies a transformation matrix."},{"order":40,"type":"bullet_point","text":"`setTransform(a, b, c, d, e, f)`: Resets the current transform to the identity matrix and then applies the transformation matrix."},{"order":41,"type":"code","text":"ctx.fillStyle = 'rgba(0, 0, 200, 0.5)'; //Semi-transparent blue
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);

ctx.translate(100, 0);
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);

ctx.rotate(Math.PI / 4); // Rotate 45 degrees
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);"},{"order":42,"type":"subheading","text":"Saving and Restoring State"},{"order":43,"type":"paragraph","text":"The `save()` and `restore()` methods allow you to save the current canvas state (transformations, styles) and restore it later. This is useful for applying temporary transformations without affecting the rest of your drawing."},{"order":44,"type":"code","text":"ctx.fillStyle = 'red';
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);

ctx.save(); // Save the current state

ctx.fillStyle = 'green';
ctx.translate(100, 0);
ctx.fillRect(10, 10, 50, 50);

ctx.restore(); // Restore the saved state

ctx.fillRect(200, 10, 50, 50); // Will be red again"},{"order":45,"type":"subheading","text":"Conclusion"},{"order":46,"type":"paragraph","text":"The HTML5 Canvas API provides a powerful and flexible way to draw graphics on the web. By mastering the methods for drawing shapes, text, and images, along with styling and transformations, you can create a wide range of visual experiences. Remember to experiment and explore the various options to unlock the full potential of the Canvas API."}]
